L["annunciation"] = """In the sixth month of Elizabeth's pregnancy, God sent the angel Gabriel to Nazareth, a small town in Galilee. He went to a young woman named Mary. She was engaged to a man named Joseph, a descendant of King David. Mary was probably a teenager, and she lived an ordinary life in a town most people overlooked.

The angel said, greetings, you who are highly favored. The Lord is with you. Mary was deeply troubled by his words and wondered what kind of greeting this was. The angel said, do not be afraid, Mary, you have found favor with God. You will conceive and give birth to a son, and you are to call him Jesus. He will be great and will be called the Son of the Most High. The Lord God will give him the throne of his father David, and he will reign over Jacob's descendants forever. His kingdom will never end.

Mary asked an honest question. How will this be, since I am a virgin? She wasn't doubting God. She wanted to understand. The angel answered, the Holy Spirit will come on you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you. So the holy one to be born will be called the Son of God. Then he gave her a sign. Your relative Elizabeth, who was thought to be unable to conceive, is going to have a child in her old age. For no word from God will ever fail.

Mary answered with one of the bravest sentences in the Bible. I am the Lord's servant. May your word to me be fulfilled. Then the angel left her.

She knew what this would cost. A young woman who was pregnant before marriage could be shamed by her whole town. Joseph could have ended the engagement. Yet she said yes. Soon after, Mary hurried to visit Elizabeth. When she arrived, Elizabeth's baby leaped inside her, and Elizabeth said, blessed is she who has believed that the Lord would fulfill his promises to her. Mary then sang a song of praise. My soul glorifies the Lord and my spirit rejoices in God my Savior.

Why does this matter? This is the moment God began to enter the world as a human being. He didn't come with an army. He came through a young woman in a small town who trusted him.

What happened after? Joseph, a good and quiet man, planned to end the engagement privately. But an angel appeared to him in a dream and told him not to be afraid to take Mary as his wife. He obeyed, and he named the child Jesus, which means the Lord saves.

What does this mean for us today? Mary didn't have the whole plan. She had a promise and a next step. She said yes before she knew how it would work out. Many of us wait to understand everything before we trust, and she shows another way. She also asked honest questions, and God didn't rebuke her for that.

That is why this story is so important. It says that nothing is impossible with God, and that he chooses humble people for great work. It is the beginning of the good news that God has come to save his people."""

L["nativity"] = """Caesar Augustus, the Roman emperor, ordered a census of the whole empire. Everyone had to go to their own town to register. Joseph was from the family line of David, so he left Nazareth and traveled about ninety miles south to Bethlehem, David's town. Mary went with him. She was about to give birth.

When they arrived, the town was crowded. While they were there, the time came for the baby to be born. She gave birth to her firstborn, a son. She wrapped him in cloths and placed him in a manger, a feeding trough for animals, because there was no guest room available for them.

That night, there were shepherds out in the fields nearby, keeping watch over their flocks. Shepherds were poor, and many people looked down on them. An angel of the Lord appeared to them, and the glory of the Lord shone around them, and they were terrified. The angel said, do not be afraid. I bring you good news that will cause great joy for all the people. Today in the town of David a Savior has been born to you. He is the Messiah, the Lord. This will be a sign to you. You will find a baby wrapped in cloths and lying in a manger.

Suddenly a great company of the heavenly host appeared with the angel, praising God and saying, glory to God in the highest heaven, and on earth peace to those on whom his favor rests.

When the angels left, the shepherds said to one another, let's go to Bethlehem and see this thing that has happened. They hurried off and found Mary and Joseph, and the baby, who was lying in the manger. After they had seen him, they spread the word about what had been told them. All who heard it were amazed. But Mary treasured up all these things and pondered them in her heart. The shepherds returned, glorifying and praising God for everything they had seen.

Why does this matter? The Creator of the universe was born as a baby in a borrowed place. The first people told were not kings or priests but shepherds. From the very beginning, the good news was for ordinary people.

What happened after? Eight days later, Jesus was named, and then Mary and Joseph took him to the temple in Jerusalem. An old man named Simeon held him and thanked God. Later, wise men from the East followed a star to find him. King Herod felt threatened, so Joseph took his family to Egypt until it was safe. Then they went home to Nazareth, where Jesus grew up.

What does this mean for us today? Christmas is not only for people who have everything in order. The message came to people working night shifts in the cold. If you feel overlooked or tired, this story says that heaven came looking for people like you. It also invites us to do what the shepherds did. They didn't just hear it. They went and saw, and then they told others.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God became one of us, and that he came for everyone. This child is the Savior, the Messiah, the Lord."""
